  "query_preview": "..\n\nCool northeast flow over Lake Huron paired with early September sun \nhas produced steep low-level lapse rates and a healthy coverage of \ndiurnal cumulus today. Benign conditions continue this evening, with \nclouds giving way to clear skies tonight as high pressure \nstrengthens and eases farther south into the region. This is driven \nby a rising geopotential height field associated with a longwave \nridge centered over the Plains. As this ridge advances east into the \nGreat Lakes, increasing stability maintains tranquil weather through \nLabor Day and into Tuesday. Clear skies and calm winds will bring \nlows into the 50s each night, with lingering low-level moisture \noffering potential for fog development. Seasonable highs in the 70s \nare expected again tomorrow, then a step up toward the lower 80s is \nforecast for Tuesday as flow emerges from the south/southeast.\n\nThe ridge flattens heading into midweek as a Pacific wave tracks \nacross the Canadian Prairie. The dynamic forcing associated with \nthis wave will hold well to the north of the region, sending a \nsurface low across northern Ontario Tuesday night into Wednesday. \nSouthwest flow ahead of the trailing cold front will bring \nincreasing moisture and relatively high confidence for showers \nTuesday night into Wednesday. A few storms will be possible as well, \nbut models continue to favor weak lapse rates and muted instability. \nBoundary-parallel flow may contribute to some training of showers, \nand deep moisture (PWAT near 1.75\" and 99th percentile integrated WV \ntransport per Euro ensemble) brings potential for heavier rainfall \nrates at times. However, the bulk of ensemble QPF output favors a \nband of heavier rain farther south over the Ohio Valley where \nstronger convection plays a role. Breezy conditions follow frontal \npassage on Thursday morning, then high confidence exists for broad \nhigh pressure to re-establish by late week, signaling the return of \npleasant early fall weather.",
